sentation model. Platform-specific integrations and third-party controls still require compatibility verification.\n\nThe .NET Core 3 migration discussion treats WPF alongside Windows Forms as an established Windows-only desktop model that can move to the newer runtime. It also presents XAML Islands as a way for WPF or Windows Forms hosts to incorporate selected UWP controls without replacing the whole application.","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Windows Desktop Applications","Windows Desktop Modernization","c8andnetcore30projectsusingazure.pdf","c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Windows Presentation Foundation on .NET Core.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Windows Presentation Foundation on .NET Core.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"windows presentation foundation on .net core windows presentation foundation on .net core","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Windows Presentation Foundation on .NET Core.md","fileName":"Windows Presentation Foundation on .NET Core.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Windows Presentation Foundation on .NET Core","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[c8andnetcore30projectsusingazure.pdf]]\n\n[[c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":[".NET Windows Desktop Applications","Windows Desktop Modernization","c8andnetcore30projectsusingazure.pdf","c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}